CNN reported on Tuesday that the classified communications network used by the National Security Agency was down, giving further fuel to a cable news headline trendline of cyber chaos.

The latest outage follows the Microsoft Outlook outage on Thursday, followed by an FAA air traffic control system glitch that grounded flights nationwide for much of the late afternoon and evening on Friday. Then there was the disruption on Monday to online banking, and a United Airline system outage later in the day.

&#8220;Tech troubles take a toll as U.S. outages pile up,&#8221; read the chyron on Fox News , which led its 11 a.m. ET hour with the past days of turmoil. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Political Ad Spending On 2025-26 Cycle Topped $12 Billion, A Boon To Broadcasters, Streaming And Digital Legal OpenAI Slapped By California AG For "Unfair & Unsafe Practices"; Sam Altman-Run Tech Firm Accuses State Of Improperly Singling Out Company

On the network on Monday, Brit Hume said, &#8220;When they mount up to this number over a short period of time like this, you have to be at least suspicious that this isn't mischief from a foreign adversary or maybe from a domestic adverary of some kind. They always say, there's no indication of terrorism or there's no indication of enemy conduct here, but I think we ought to wait a while before we come to any final conclusions on that.&#8221; Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

But commenters wondered if there was some kind of connection between the outages, the White House has been downplaying the reports. On his trip to Asia, Donald Trump told reporters, &#8220;It's life. When you're big and powerful and you have all the money, things like that happen, it's fine.&#8221;

Citing unnamed sources, CNN reported that there was no evidence of a cyberattack in the case of the NSA outage.

Also on Monday, the security platform Report URI reported that some 178 companies and organizations, including Zoom, the BBC, CNN and Peacock, were exposed to malicious JavaScript via the Optimizely Web experimentation platform. Also impacted, per the report, were Salesforce, IKEA and Equifax, among others, but most of the code was removed by the end of the day. A spokesperson for Optimizely did not immediate respond to a request for comment.

On CNN, Chris Krebs, former director of the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, was a bit circumspect when trying to connect the dots between the various outages. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

He said, &#8220;I think more than anything, what it shows is virtually every aspect of our lives are now digitally connected. We have so many things that rely on functioning connected digital services, and those underlying services, whether they're provided by a large vendor or a small vendor, might be a little rickety. Might be based on two decades worth of technology, and in some cases, some technical debt that needs to be updated.&#8221;

He added, &#8220;AI is adding a complexity here because we're using AI to find vulnerabilities in softwares and systems, and we're patching systems at a rate far beyond anything in the past. Even just the last six months alone, we're finding vulnerabilities and patching at just an absolutely insane pace. And anytime you do a patch, anytime you roll an update, it can introduce it can destabilize the system.&#8221;

Meanwhile, at 2:20 p.m. ET on Tuesday, TV viewers will be treated to a test of the emergency alert system, conducted across broadcasters, cable systems, satellite radio and wireline video providers, per the FCC. The timing may be disconcerting right on the heels of the outages, but it's required by law at least once every three years. Viewers also will be fully warned: It's a test, and it's only a test.

Skydance Motion Picture Group co-chairs Dana Goldberg and Josh Greenstein have set their leadership team.

Kira Goldberg and Don Granger will serve as president of Paramount's Motion Picture Group, and Jesse Ehrmann has been elevated to president of Warner Bros. Motion Picture Group. Michael Ireland will continue as president of production, reporting to Ehrmann.

Shaun Barber and Mark Viane will oversee domestic and international distribution, respectively for both Paramount and Warner Bros. With their promotions, Jeff Goldstein, a 48-year veteran of Warner Bros. Pictures who was most recently the head of global distribution, will retire. He's expected to transition to a consulting role. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The USC Norman Lear Center has teamed up with environmental group Good Energy for a deep dive on how Hollywood is addressing climate change .

"The Evolution of Climate Onscreen&#8221; report, released on Tuesday, tracks how conversations around climate change show up in scripted entertainment. The study looked at 35,120 scripts between 2021 and 2024, an update to its last report that looked at film and TV between 2016 and 2020. (Therefore a major climate project like Digger , from earlier this year , was not included in the research window). blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Lifestyle Joel Kim Booster to Host Jackson Wild and Hollywood Climate Summit Media Awards Movies Ben Affleck Warns 'Digger' Box Office Bomb Will Lead to "A Bunch of Sh***y Homogenized Movies"

The new report finds that climate-related keywords appeared in 3.2 percent of all scripts between 2021-2024, a 14 percent increase from the 2016-2020 findings . Across 100 storylines where climate was prominent, 87 percent featured climate impacts like rising sea levels, bad air, food stress or biodiversity loss; 72 percent highlighted climate change through disasters, mostly floods, extreme heat and hurricanes or storms; and 66 percent portrayed causes of climate change such as plastics, industrial agriculture, deforestation or fossil fuels.

Additionally, climate solutions appeared in 10 percent of storylines, and nearly three times as many highlighted individual consumer behaviors over clean energy or other large-scale solutions. Medical and romance were found as the top two genres for climate storytelling to appear.

"Climate is becoming more visible in film and television, but counting references can only tell us so much. For the first time, we have a map of the stories themselves. Disasters dominate, but nearly two-thirds of the climate storylines we studied also include causes, now bringing the human choices and systems behind the crisis into view,&#8221; Good Energy founder and CEO Anna Jane Joyner said in a statement. &#8220;At their center are complicated people &#x2014; what they love, what they stand to lose, how they respond individually and together, and the fear, grief, humor, courage and wonder that emerge along the way. What thrills me is the rich creative territory artists are already exploring, and all that still lies ahead as they examine what it means to be human in the age of climate change.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Added USC Norman Lear Center director of research Erica Rosenthal, &#8220;We were honored to re-team with Good Energy on this groundbreaking research. In addition to tracking an increase in discussions of climate change over the last several years, we did a deep-dive into the themes and context of 100 climate stories. We found nearly three in four portrayed or mentioned disasters, but over the course of these stories, the dominant tone shifted from threat toward human agency.&#8221;

&#8220;This is the first study to look at both how often climate comes up in Hollywood's scripts and how it's actually portrayed on screen. While modest in volume, climate visibility is growing in depth and range,&#8221; said Good Energy head of narrative research and strategy Bruno Olmedo-Quiroga. &#8220;Across more than 35,000 scripts, the climate conversation came up in about one in seven, on every major network and streamer, in comedies, romances and even horror. Writers are already bringing climate into every kind of story. And we now know what they portray, the characters at their center, and how their emotional journeys unfold.&#8221;

Amelia Dimoldenberg is breaking up with &#8220; Chicken Shop Date .&#8221;

After 12 years, the beloved interview series &#x2014; in which Dimoldenberg awkwardly attempts to flirt with some of the biggest celebrities in the world, from Andrew Garfield to Cher &#x2014; is coming to an end next week. To bid the show goodbye, the host, writer and producer is releasing a film version of the final season, which will screen in theaters on Nov. 24 before the last episode drops on YouTube Nov. 27. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Brandi Carlile's All-Requests Tour Isn't Just a Magnificent Stunt, but an Unusually Moving Transaction Between Performer and Audience: Concert Review

When she started the show, Dimoldenberg was a &#8220;17-year-old who had never gone on a date before &#8230; I know, it's shocking,&#8221; she told the audience at the world premiere of the &#8220;Chicken Shop Date&#8221; movie Monday night in London. &#8220;Now, I'm going through a massive break-up, so pray for me.&#8221;

In a Q&A with presenter Lea Ogunlami before the movie &#x2014; which contains interviews with Role Model, Jorja Smith, Noel Gallagher, Nemzzz, Jack O'Connell and a secret final guest &#x2014; Dimoldenberg reminisced on her favorite memories from the chicken shop.

&#8220;I know an episode's going to be huge if they flirt with me,&#8221; she said when asked if there's a formula to which dates go viral. &#8220;If they flirt with me back, it's going to be a win. But a lot of the time they don't, which is kind of annoying.&#8221;

Asked to name the flirtiest celebs, Dimoldenberg revealed: &#8220;Damson Idris, I think that's my mom's favorite one. Andrew Garfield, obviously. Billie Eilish. Daniel Kaluuya.&#8221;

The guest who made her laugh the hardest was comedian Eric Andr&#xE9;, and she had her most awkward moment with supermodel Bella Hadid. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;I asked Bella Hadid, &#8216;What is it like to be so beautiful?' And it just was really awkward,&#8221; she said with a laugh. &#8220;It honestly was. I thought that would be a really funny one but &#8230; I edited it out.&#8221;

Dimoldenberg is excited for fans to get to experience the show in a communal setting for the first time when the final episodes hit theaters in the U.S., U.K., Ireland and France.

&#8220;This is my first experience, actually right now, of seeing people come together and watch it,&#8221; she said. &#8220;And it's going to be new for me to watch people watch the show. Obviously, I'm hoping people laugh.&#8221;

But she's forever grateful to YouTube for giving her a platform to grow her career. &#8220;I first pitched the show to TV broadcasters and they all said no, and so then I was like, &#8216;OK, I'll put it on YouTube,'&#8221; Dimoldenberg said. &#8220;I am where I am today because of YouTube and the fact that anyone with access to the internet and an idea can upload and create a show, a brand, an audience.&#8221;

As for what's next, Dimoldenberg &#x2014; who has a feature film and drama series in the works &#x2014; said the most important aspect is that she retains creative control, as she has with &#8220;Chicken Shop Date&#8221; through her production company Dimz Inc.

&#8220;I'm taking time to write, and I feel like co-producing something is always going to be the thing that I want to do, again to make sure that I can have as much control as possible,&#8221; she said. &#8220;There are so many different things that can happen in the future, and I'm excited.&#8221;

Kira Goldberg is departing Netflix where she has been for eight years, most recently as VP Film, Faith, Holiday, YA, and Family. She is headed to Paramount Pictures where she will take on a senior position alongside Don Granger. Both will be Presidents, Motion Picture Group, reporting to Dana Goldberg and Josh Greenstein, Co-Chairs, Motion Picture Group at Skydance.

Word about Goldberg's departure started spreading Tuesday morning when she notified the staff, sources said.

